===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|en|steam|er}}. The {{m|en|steamer duck}} or ''steamer (sense 6.2)'' is apparently named for its resemblance to a [[paddle steamer]] when swimming rapidly.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{non-gloss definition|A [[device]] or [[object#Noun|object]] that [[work#Verb|works]] by the [[operation]] of [[steam#Noun|steam]].}}
## A [[vessel]] in which [[article]]s are [[subject#Verb|subjected]] to the action of steam, as in [[washing]] and in various [[process#Noun|processes]] of [[manufacture#Noun|manufacture]].
## {{lb|en|cookware}} A [[cook]]ing [[appliance]] that cooks by steaming.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A steam [[fire engine]], that is, a [[steam boiler]] and [[engine]] driving a [[pump#Noun|pump]], which are all [[mount#Verb|mounted]] on [[wheel#Noun|wheels]].
# {{non-gloss definition|A mode of [[transportation]] [[propel]]led by steam.}}
## {{lb|en|rail transport}} A steam-powered [[road]] [[locomotive]]; a [[traction engine]].
## {{lb|en|nautical}} A vessel propelled by steam; a [[steamboat]] or [[steamship]].
##* '''1859''' January 6, “English and Amer[i]can navies”, in ''[[w:Olney Times|The Olney Times]]'' (reprinted from the ''{{w|New York Herald}}''), volume 3, number 27 (number 130 overall), Olney, Ill.: William F. Beck, <small>{{OCLC|23245310}}</small>, [http://www.newspapers.com/clip/3066723/english_and_american_navies/ page 1, column 6]:
##*: Her gunboat fleet alone is composed of one hundred and sixty-one small '''steamers''', of the efficiency of which for war purports naval critics have formed a high estimate.
#* {{RQ:Wharton Marne|chapter=I|page=1|passage=Every since the age of six Troy Belknap of New York had embarked for Europe every June on the fastest '''steamer''' of one of the most expensive lines. With his family he had descended at the dock from a large noiseless motor, had kissed his father good-bye, turned back to shake hands with the chauffeur (a particular friend), and trotted up the gang-plank behind his mother's maid, [...]}}
# {{clipping of|en|steamer trunk}}
# A [[babycino]] ([[frothy]] [[milk#Noun|milk]] [[drink#Noun|drink]]).
# A [[wetsuit]] with long [[sleeve]]s and [[leg]]s.
# {{non-gloss definition|The name of various [[animal]]s.}}
## The {{vern|soft-shell clam}}, {{vern|sand gaper}}, or {{vern|long-neck clam}} (''[[Mya arenaria]]''), an [[edible]] [[saltwater]] [[clam#Noun|clam]]; ''specifically'' the clam when [[steam#Verb|steamed]] for [[eating#Noun|eating]].
## A [[steamer duck]]: any of the four [[species]] of the [[duck#Noun|duck]] [[genus]] ''[[Tachyeres]]'' which are all found in [[South America]], three of which are [[flightless]].
# {{lb|en|horse racing}} A [[racehorse]] the [[odds]] of which are becoming shorter (that is, decreasing) because [[bettor]]s are [[back#Verb|backing]] it.
# {{lb|en|Britain|slang}} An act of [[fellatio]].
# {{lb|en|Britain|crime|slang}} A member of a [[youth#Adjective|youth]] [[gang]] who engages in [[steaming#Noun|steaming]] ([[rob]]bing and [[escape|escaping]] in a large group).
# {{lb|en|Britain|Scotland|slang}} A [[drinking]] [[session]].
# {{lb|en|Australia|obsolete}} A [[dish#Noun|dish]] made by cooking [[diced]] [[meat]] very slowly in a tightly [[sealed]] [[pot#Noun|pot]] with a minimum of [[flavouring#Noun|flavourings]], allowing it to [[steam#Verb|steam]] in its own [[juice#Noun|juices]]; ''specifically'' such a dish made with [[kangaroo]] meat. {{defdate|{{circa2|1850|short=yes}} – 1900s.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Henry [Saxelby] Melville|authorlink=Henry Melville|chapter=XII|title=The Present State of Australia, including New South Wales, Western Australia, South Australia, Victoria and New Zealand, with Practical Hints on Emigration; to which are Added the Land Regulations, and Description of the Aborigines and Their Habits|location=London|publisher=G. Willis, Great Piazza, {{w|Covent Garden}}|year=1851|page=311|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8hEOAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA311|oclc=154636947|passage=But of all the dishes ever brought to table, nothing equals that of the "'''steamer'''." It is made by mincing the flesh of the kangaroo, and with it some pieces of pork or bacon. The animal has not any fat, or scarcely any, in its best season; when the meat is chopped up, it is thrown into a saucepan and covered over with the lid, and left to stew or steam gently by the fire-side: it is, from this method of cooking, called "'''steamer'''." [...] the flesh soon floats in its own rich gravy: it only requires pepper and salt to render it delicious. No one can tell what a '''steamer''' is, unless it has been tasted; it indeed affords an excellent repast, and it is surprising that the '''steamer''', preserved in tins, has not yet been exported to England.}}

